From b13fe98104f5332dac6b30593aa837d74dc123dc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dimitry B <dimitryB@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 00:15:58 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08/13] eip155: verify EIP-1271 signatures before the transfer,
 and report signature reverts

A payer with code (a contract wallet) and a signature the wallet does not
accept got HTTP 500 unexpected_verify_error / unexpected_settle_error: the
signature was only checked inside the transfer simulation or gas estimate,
whose revert ("FiatTokenV2: invalid signature", Permit2
InvalidContractSignature()) surfaced as a transport error.

- For an EIP-1271 signature, after checking that the payer has code, call the
  payer's isValidSignature(hash, signature) with the EIP-712 hash the token or
  Permit2 will check, and require the magic value 0x1626ba7e. Any other answer
  or a revert is an invalid signature; only a failure to reach the chain is
  reported as such. This mirrors the TS reference facilitator's strict EIP-1271
  check and runs before balances and allowances are read.
- When a transfer simulation or send still fails, recognise signature reverts:
  an Error(string) mentioning an invalid signature, or Permit2's
  InvalidSignature(), InvalidSigner(), InvalidContractSignature() and
  InvalidSignatureLength(). They are reported as invalid signatures, i.e.
  invalid_exact_evm_payload_signature, which also covers an EIP-712 domain in
  the requirements that differs from the token's and EIP-6492 wallets.
- explain_used_eip3009_authorization / explain_used_permit2_nonce are renamed
  explain_failed_eip3009_transfer / explain_failed_permit2_transfer, since they
  now explain more than replays.

EIP-6492 counterfactual wallets are unchanged: their signatures are still
checked by the validator during the simulation.
